"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Hoisin sauce is a thick, savory sauce originating in China. It features in many Chinese cuisines, but is most prominent in Cantonese cuisine. It can be used as a glaze for meat, an addition to stir fry, or as dipping sauce. It is dark-coloured, sweet and salty. Although regional variants exist, hoisin sauce usually includes soybeans, fennel, red chili peppers, and garlic. Vinegar, five-spice powder, and sugar are also commonly added."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-hoisin-sauce"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hoisin_sauce"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Char siu","dish/char-siu"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Five-spice powder","ingredient/five-spice-powder"],["Fortified wine","ingredient/fortified-wine"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Maltose","ingredient/maltose"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Rice wine","ingredient/rice-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d1/Hoisin_sauce_1.jpg/500px-Hoisin_sauce_1.jpg","w":500,"h":434,"by":"allen watkin from London, UK","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoisin_sauce_1.jpg"}},"ingredient/grape-juice":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/grape-juice","n":"Grape juice","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Grape juice is obtained from crushing and blending grapes into a liquid. In the wine industry, grape juice that contains 7–23 percent of pulp, skins, stems and seeds is often referred to as must. The sugars in grape juice allow it to be used as a sweetener, fermented, and made into wine, brandy, or vinegar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-grape-juice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grape_juice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Churchkhela","dish/churchkhela"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Must","ingredient/must"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_grape_juice.jpeg/500px-Glass_of_grape_juice.jpeg","w":500,"h":559,"by":"Oyp","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_of_grape_juice.jpeg"}},"dish/jjolmyeon":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jjolmyeon","n":"Jjolmyeon","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from South Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Jjolmyeon is either a type of Korean noodle with a very chewy texture made from wheat flour and starch, or a cold and spicy dish bibim-jjolmyeon (비빔쫄면) made with the noodles and vegetables. Jjolmyeon can add many vegetables such as cabbage and bean sprouts. The spicy and hot sauce is a combination of gochujang (chili pepper paste), vinegar, sugar, and minced garlic. It is also a type of bibim guksu (mixed noodles)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jjolmyeon"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jjolmyeon"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Gochujang","ingredient/gochujang"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from South Korean cuisine","links":[["Chalbori-ppang","dish/chalbori-ppang"],["Chimaek","dish/chimaek"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dak-galbi","dish/dak-galbi"],["Gilgeori toast","dish/gilgeori-toast"],["Hodu-gwaja","dish/hodu-gwaja"],["Hwangnam-ppang","dish/hwangnam-ppang"],["Jinju bibimbap","dish/jinju-bibimbap"],["Kkulppang","dish/kkulppang"],["Milmyeon","dish/milmyeon"],["Rabokki","dish/rabokki"],["So-tteok-so-tteok","dish/so-tteok-so-tteok"],["Tornado potato","dish/tornado-potato"],["Ttongppang","dish/ttongppang"],["Yangnyeom chicken","dish/yangnyeom-chicken"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/ba/Boiled_jjolmyeon.jpg/500px-Boiled_jjolmyeon.jpg","w":500,"h":383,"by":"딸기맘양갱이","lic":"CC BY 2.0 kr","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/kr/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boiled_jjolmyeon.jpg"}},"dish/sausage-sizzle":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/sausage-sizzle","n":"Sausage sizzle","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Australian cuisine","dot":"#55b8ad","intro":[["A sausage sizzle is a community event originating from either Australia or New Zealand to cook and serve sausages in bread (also referred to as sausage sandwiches or sausage sizzles), which are grilled or barbecued sausages (most commonly beef or pork) served in sliced bread with grilled onions and various condiments, most commonly tomato sauce, barbecue sauce or mustard. The term \"sausage sizzle\" came into common use in the 1980s and is used primarily to refer to the barbecuing event, but also to the sausage itself, mostly in Western Australia."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-sausage-sizzle"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sausage_sizzle"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Australian cuisine","cuisine/australian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Sausage","ingredient/sausage"],["Sliced bread","ingredient/sliced-bread"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Belly Rumbles: Australian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"bellyrumbles.com","note":"Sara McCleary's collection of Australian classics such as Anzac biscuits, pavlova, sausage rolls and native-ingredient bakes.","url":"https://bellyrumbles.com/category/australian-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"RecipeTin Eats","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"recipetineats.com","note":"Sydney-based Nagi Maehashi's widely used recipe site; broad in scope, but a staple of Australian home cooking.","url":"https://www.recipetineats.com/home/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Australian cuisine","links":[["Anzac biscuit","dish/anzac-biscuit"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Flake (fish)","dish/flake-fish"],["Kangaroo meat","dish/kangaroo-meat"],["Parrot pie","dish/parrot-pie"],["Pavlova","dish/pavlova"],["Steak and eggs","dish/steak-and-eggs"],["Zucchini slice","dish/zucchini-slice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d5/Sausage_Sizzle_-_Snowy_Valley_Resort_AUD3.50.jpg/500px-Sausage_Sizzle_-_Snowy_Valley_Resort_AUD3.50.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Alpha","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sausage_Sizzle_-_Snowy_Valley_Resort_AUD3.50.jpg"}},"ingredient/soybeans":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/soybeans","n":"Soybeans","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["The soybean, soy bean, or soya bean (Glycine max) is a species of legume native to East Asia, widely grown for its edible bean. Soy is a staple crop, the world's most grown legume, and an important animal feed."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-soybeans"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soybeans"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",[